  <a href="#ownership" id="ownership" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Who owns the delivery lifecycle?</h2>
  <section><p class="wide">Each option below identifies who runs the work, manages the connection and closes resources.
    Choose one path for a delivery flow. For the underlying module relationships, see <a href="#layers">the architecture diagram</a>.</p></section>

  <a href="#option-no-pool" id="option-no-pool" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>1. Reuse one connection without a pool</h2>
  <section class="pooling-option">
    <div class="view">
      <div class="pooling-option-meta"><span class="pooling-owner">Owner: Simple Java Mail</span><span>Sequential</span><span>No clustering</span></div>
      <p>
        Your overnight job sends a set of reports one after another. You can take SMTP connection setup out of that loop.
        Pass a collection or lazy iterable to <code>sendMailsInSimpleBatch(...)</code>, or use
        <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code> if you want to decide what to send inside a callback. With a single route,
        both open a dedicated connection and close it when the work finishes.
      </p>
      <div class="pooling-benefit">
        <strong>This gives you</strong>
        <p>
          One connection is enough for a bounded, sequential run. A pool is useful when independent sends overlap or several
          SMTP Sessions form a cluster.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Dependency:</strong> <code>org.simplejavamail:simple-java-mail:{{site.version}}</code></p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">try (Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user", "secret")
        .buildMailer()) {
    mailer.withOpenConnection(sender -> {
        for (Email email : emails) {
            sender.sendMail(email);
        }
    });
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>
        If you already have the reports in an iterable, call <code>mailer.sync().sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails)</code>.
        It reads the iterable as it sends and returns without collecting a list of receipts.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With failover configured:</strong> these same operations follow the
        <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-batches">selected failover route</a>. With the batch module present, they retain a lease from the
        selected pool, occupying capacity until they release it; a healthy release need not close the socket. Without that module, they retain a direct
        connection. The callback stays on its selected connection, while a simple batch can switch between untouched emails.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#option-mailer" id="option-mailer" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>2. Let Simple Java Mail orchestrate pooled sends</h2>
  <section class="pooling-option">
    <div class="view">
      <div class="pooling-option-meta"><span class="pooling-owner">Owner: Simple Java Mail</span><span>Automatic leases</span><span>Clusters</span></div>
      <p>
        Sign-up confirmations and receipts can arrive at the same time. If both already use an <code>Email</code> builder and a
        <code>Mailer</code>, add <code>batch-module</code> and let the Mailer borrow a connection for each send. It chooses the Session,
        builds the message for that route, and returns or discards the connection after the send.
      </p>
      <div class="pooling-benefit">
        <strong>This gives you</strong>
        <p>
          The same Mailer handles background work and pool shutdown. This also works when your routes use a cluster of registered Sessions.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Dependencies:</strong> <code>simple-java-mail:{{site.version}}</code> plus <code>batch-module:{{site.version}}</code></p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">try (Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user", "secret")
        .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
            .withCoreSize(2)
            .withMaxSize(10)
            .withExpireAfter(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
            .build())
        .buildMailer()) {
    mailer.sync().sendMail(email); // or mailer.async().sendMail(email) for background work
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <ul class="pooling-checklist">
        <li>A default executor belongs to the Mailer; a supplied executor remains caller-owned.</li>
        <li>The Mailer chooses the actual clustered Session and builds the message for it.</li>
        <li><code>Mailer.close()</code> waits for accepted work and closes that Mailer's registered pool.</li>
        <li>Retry, circuit breaking, and ambiguous-delivery policy remain application concerns.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#option-batch-facade" id="option-batch-facade" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>3. Use batch-module without SimpleJavaMail or Mailer</h2>
  <section class="pooling-option">
    <div class="view">
      <div class="pooling-option-meta"><span class="pooling-owner">Owner: BatchTransportExecutor</span><span>Callbacks</span><span>CompletableFuture</span></div>
      <p>
        Maybe your report service already builds <code>MimeMessage</code> objects and you want to keep that code.
        Give each unit of work to <code>BatchTransportExecutor&lt;K&gt;</code>. It selects a Session, lends the callback
        a connected Transport from <code>smtp-connection-pool</code>, and returns a <code>CompletableFuture</code> for submitted work.
      </p>
      <div class="pooling-benefit">
        <strong>This gives you</strong>
        <p>
          Your application decides what to do in the callback. The executor owns the lease and keeps track of accepted work until shutdown.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Dependency:</strong> <code>org.simplejavamail:batch-module:{{site.version}}</code>. The main <code>simple-java-mail</code> facade is not required.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;batch-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;{{site.version}}&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java">Properties properties = new Properties();
properties.setProperty("mail.smtp.host", "smtp.example.com");
properties.setProperty("mail.smtp.port", "587");
properties.setProperty("mail.smtp.auth", "true");

Session session = Session.getInstance(properties, new Authenticator() {
    protected PasswordAuthentication getPasswordAuthentication() {
        return new PasswordAuthentication("user", "secret");
    }
});

try (BatchTransportExecutor&lt;String&gt; batch =
             BatchTransportExecutor.&lt;String&gt;builder()
                     .withMaxPoolSize(8)
                     .withClaimTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
                     .withExpireAfter(Duration.ofMinutes(1))
                     .build()) {
    batch.registerSession("transactional", session);

    CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt; sent = batch.submit("transactional",
            (selectedSession, transport) -> {
                MimeMessage message = new MimeMessage(selectedSession);
                message.setFrom("sender@example.com");
                message.setRecipients(Message.RecipientType.TO, "recipient@example.com");
                message.setSubject("Queued report");
                message.setText("The report is ready.");
                transport.sendMessage(message, message.getAllRecipients());
                return null;
            });
    sent.join();
}</code></pre>
      <p>If another service handed you a <code>MimeMessage</code> already built for <code>session</code>, borrow from that exact pool.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">batch.execute("transactional", session, (selectedSession, transport) -> {
    transport.sendMessage(existingMessage, existingMessage.getAllRecipients());
    return null;
});</code></pre>
            <h3>Follow one callback through shutdown</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Select the Session.</strong> Build the message with the Session the cluster selects. If an existing message was built
          for a specific registered Session, use the exact-Session call above.</li>
        <li><strong>Send with the borrowed Transport.</strong> Leave it open. Returning normally releases it. An escaping checked exception,
          runtime exception or error invalidates it and propagates the original failure.</li>
        <li><strong>Close the executor from its owning thread.</strong> The try-with-resources block above waits for accepted callbacks and
          closes the pools and internally created worker executor. Keep any executor you supplied running until callbacks finish.</li>
      </ol>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Shutdown futures, forced shutdown and callback restrictions</summary>
<ul>        <li><strong>Close from the thread that owns the executor.</strong> Use try-with-resources on the owning thread, as above. Graceful shutdown waits for accepted callbacks and closes the pools and any
          executor the batch created. Keep a supplied executor running until those callbacks finish, then shut it down yourself. A worker
          startup <code>Error</code> that prevents a callback from starting propagates and leaves nothing for graceful shutdown to wait on.</li>
        <li><strong>Track cleanup separately from each shutdown call.</strong> Each call to <code>shutdown()</code> or <code>shutdownNow()</code> returns its own view of cleanup. You can cancel, complete, or time
          out one view and still observe full cleanup through another call or <code>close()</code>. Use <code>shutdownNow()</code> to escalate
          a graceful shutdown. Application code that ignores interruption can still hold up completion.</li>
        <li><strong>Start shutdown from a callback if needed.</strong> A callback or inline completion handler can initiate <code>shutdown()</code> or <code>shutdownNow()</code> and return. Let the
          owner wait outside that work. Calling blocking <code>close()</code> there throws <code>IllegalStateException</code> before it starts
          shutdown, even in a continuation like <code>thenRun(batch::close)</code> on the completing worker.
          See the <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#async-shutdown">migration notes</a>.</li>
</ul>
</details>

    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#option-direct-pool" id="option-direct-pool" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>4. Claim leases from smtp-connection-pool directly</h2>
  <section class="pooling-option">
    <div class="view">
      <div class="pooling-option-meta"><span class="pooling-owner">Owner: Application</span><span>Explicit lease</span><span>Maximum control</span></div>
      <p>
        If your delivery service already has its own worker queue, tenant routing, retry policy, and metrics, plug in just the SMTP pool.
        Choose the Session and claim a lease when a worker is ready to send. Simple Java Mail's Mailer uses the same direct pool API internally.
      </p>
      <div class="pooling-benefit">
        <strong>This gives you</strong>
        <p>
          Your code decides when to claim, return or invalidate each lease, and when to stop workers and drain the pool.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Dependency:</strong> <code>org.simplejavamail:smtp-connection-pool:4.5.0</code></p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">SmtpConnectionPool pool =
        new SmtpConnectionPool(new SmtpClusterConfig&lt;Session&gt;());

try (SmtpTransportLease lease = pool.claimTransport(session)) {
    try {
        lease.getTransport().sendMessage(message, message.getAllRecipients());
    } catch (MessagingException | RuntimeException failure) {
        lease.invalidate();
        throw failure;
    }
}

pool.shutDown().get();</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>
        The try-with-resources close releases an active lease. Invalidate first when connection health is uncertain. Your code owns interruption policy,
        active-work tracking, shutdown waiting, and every path that could otherwise leak a lease.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the relay uses short-lived access tokens, register the supplier before the first claim. It belongs to that Session's exact pool.
        The pool asks for a token when it opens or reconnects a physical Transport. Use a constant supplier for a fixed token.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">ResourcePoolKey&lt;Session&gt; key = new ResourcePoolKey&lt;&gt;(session);
pool.registerResourcePool(key, tokenService::currentAccessToken);</code></pre>
      <p>
        Pass <code>null</code> as the second argument at initial registration to use ordinary Session authentication and ignore legacy
        pool OAuth properties. An active pool keeps the credential choice it was registered with. Registrations without the authentication
        argument and implicit claims still honor the legacy properties.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#option-provider" id="option-provider" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>5. Present the pool as a Jakarta Mail Transport</h2>
  <section class="pooling-option">
    <div class="view">
      <div class="pooling-option-meta"><span class="pooling-owner">Owner: smtppool provider</span><span>Jakarta Mail</span><span>Spring</span></div>
      <p>
        If your existing sender opens and closes a Jakarta Mail <code>Transport</code>, use the <code>smtppool</code> provider.
        Each <code>connect()</code> borrows a physical connection and <code>close()</code> returns or discards it. Spring's
        <code>JavaMailSenderImpl</code> can use the same provider.
      </p>
      <div class="pooling-benefit">
        <strong>This gives you</strong>
        <p>
          Your Jakarta Mail or Spring code keeps building messages and deciding when they run. The provider takes care of the leases.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Dependencies:</strong> <code>smtp-connection-pool-jakarta-provider:4.5.0</code> and one physical provider such as <code>angus-mail:2.0.5</code></p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">Properties properties = new Properties();
properties.setProperty(SmtpPoolProperties.DELEGATE_PROTOCOL, "smtp");
Session session = Session.getInstance(properties);

Transport transport = session.getTransport("smtppool");
transport.connect("smtp.example.com", 587, "user", "secret");
try {
    transport.sendMessage(message, message.getAllRecipients());
} finally {
    transport.close(); // release healthy; invalidate unhealthy
}

SmtpPoolRegistry.shutdown(session).get();</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>
        Spring configures the same provider with <code>JavaMailSenderImpl#setProtocol("smtppool")</code>. Application/framework executors and queued work
        remain outside the provider; the Session-scoped registry owns pool draining and forced escalation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#option-camel" id="option-camel" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>6. Let Camel select the pooled provider</h2>
  <section class="pooling-option">
    <div class="view">
      <div class="pooling-option-meta"><span class="pooling-owner">Owner: Camel + provider</span><span>Routes</span><span>Java 17+</span></div>
      <p>
        If a Camel route already turns exchanges into email, switch its SMTP endpoint to <code>smtppool:</code> or <code>smtppools:</code>.
        Camel keeps the routes, exchanges, and error handling. The adapter selects the same pooled Jakarta Mail provider.
      </p>
      <div class="pooling-benefit">
        <strong>This gives you</strong>
        <p>
          When the component stops, it shuts down pools for Sessions it owns.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Dependency:</strong> <code>org.simplejavamail:smtp-connection-pool-camel:4.5.0</code></p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">from("direct:mail")
    .to("smtppool://smtp.example.com:587"
        + "?username=user"
        + "&amp;password=secret"
        + "&amp;to=recipient@example.com");</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>
        Camel 4.21.x and the adapter require Java 17+. The original direct pool and Jakarta provider remain Java 8 compatible; the Simple Java Mail batch facade follows the Java 11 library baseline.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#layers" id="layers" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2 id="pooling-layers-title">One application, six delivery paths</h2>
  {{> components/pattern-examples topic="tenant-routing"}}
  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      This page explains which integration owns messages, transports, and pools. For Simple Java Mail's own Mailer queue limits,
      message/recipient sending rates, deadlines, cancellation, observer dispatch, and shutdown, see <a href="/sending-and-execution.html">Sending and execution</a>.
      Those settings do not configure standalone BatchTransportExecutor operations or another framework's sends.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">Pooling manages ordinary capacity and load balancing. <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html">Connection failover</a> handles
      eligible failures and recovery through configured Mailer relationships. A full but healthy pool keeps waiting normally; it doesn't spill work
      elsewhere. In either case, the selected member owns ordinary Email templates and SMTP settings, while the first registration still owns shared pool settings.</p>
    <p class="wide">
      Every option starts in your application. Follow a solid green path for the programming model you want to keep; dashed links reveal which modules
      provide the physical pool underneath. Path 01 reuses one scoped connection without a pool. Paths 02 through 06 converge on the same
      <code>smtp-connection-pool</code> foundation.
    </p>

